From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: The IQ Fetish

Ed Lane writes some more about those who are apparently "off the scale":

>....Those of you who are "off the scale" for standard
>IQ tests must seek some other measurement tool (Lefty, Eric).  

I think we need to expand the "HEx" system into an "IQ capital" system.
Instead of Robin's "Polite Society," we could have the "Intelligent
Society." Membership would be based on a combination of Stanford-Binet IQ
scores, SAT scores, Wechsler scores, anecdotal reports of one's genius
level, and so on. Only the postings of the "provably intelligent" would be
read.

Initial membership will of course be confined to just a select few who can
document their claims to genius level intelligence.

...
>More at some future date.  Sorry to leave a thread just when it's
>getting started but I must now apply my limited brain power to some
>"real world" problems.  (deadlines approaching)

Wait, Ed, as keeper of the list of Intelligent Persons, you can't leave
this thread now! We outsiders seek "eternal verities" from Marilyn, Lefty,
and Eric.

SARCASM MODE OFF: You can see where I think this crap of announcing IQ
scores leads. I've seen this happen in too many groups (mostly not here on
the Net, thankfully.) First someone announces, often casually, their GRE
scores, or their 800s in math, or their "off the scale" IQ (often with an
anecdote about how it scared the teachers and the horses and caused
ostracism and poor socialization), and then others chime in. A kind of "IQ
flame war." Very predictable and very boring. Also very alienating to most
folks (including bright ones).

The "Extropians" list, by its principles and its stated focus on
intelligence and intelligence enhancement, is unusually prone to this sort
of trend. Thankfully, several longterm members of the list have pointed out
the futility of this.

And in fairness to the list members mentioned above, Lefty and Eric, they
did not proclaim themselves to be smarter than the rest of us, they just
gave their IQ anecdotes. Maybe they *are* smarter than the rest of us--who
cares?

What I care about is the quality of the thoughts expressed here on this
list, not the so-called IQs of others, whether active posters or lurkers.

As for people who want to boost their "IQs"--fine, give it your best shot.
I'm a lot more interested in using my current intelligence as best I can
than in increasing my score a few points on a test which I'll never again
have to take.


-Tim May





