Date: Sat, 3 Apr 93 17:15:07 -0800
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: I**2:  Brain Tuning (getting smart the old fashioned way)

Some comments on Ed Lane's specific points:

>When I begin to optimize an application I do some "profiling" and seek
>to identify the "bottle-necks" in the system (forgive my use of CS
>terminology, I'm sure that instructional psychologists have their own
>words for this).  I am usually dealing with "well-understood" systems.
>Black-Box optimization is somewhat different however.  This is how I
>would characterize my understanding of my own brain at this time.  By
>"tweaking" the various mental skills we can observe what influence they
>have on the measured result-- IQ.  From this we can draw conclusions
>about the importance of certain skills and their relative contribution
>in overall intelligence.  This approach is intuitively appealing to me
>as I have had success with it in dealing with many "undocumented"
>systems.

I think Ed needs to study some statistics before designing his "tweak"
experiments.

We just don't have IQ tests, whether Stanford-Binet or SAT scores or
whatever, that can give us this kind of feedback on a reasonable time
scale. Most such tests have several characteristics which would make them
flawed for the kind of self-improvement Ed Lane writes about:

* they take several hours to complete and cannot easily and fairly be
self-administered. The Mensa and International Legion of Intelligence tests
are jokes. An SAT session takes several hours, and is still subject to
large amounts of scatter in the results--scatter which would of course
swamp all but the most striking changes in intelligence. So much for seeing
"tweaks."

(For example: Take an SAT test five or more times. Average the results and
compute the standard deviation (itself subject to noise, scatter, of
course). Apply the "tweak." Repeat the test five more times. Take average,
std. dev., etc. Correct for the increase expected due to experience and the
*decrease* due to boredom and weariness, and then look for a difference. A
tough challenge. And there are some flaws in this methodology you could
drive a truck through! Then repeat for the next tweak, etc. Now clearly
this sort of experiment might be possible and even interesting with
statistically large samples of students, but not when self-administered!)

* to see an effect of a "tweak" in this very "noisy" environment would be a
challenge indeed.

I haven't heard of any evidence that innate intelligence, whatever it is,
can be significantly changed, at least not past a certain age (I'm
sidestepping the nature vs. nurture debate for now). Smart drugs do not
seem to have much effect (a topic we've debated on this list at least 3
times since last summer), nor do IQ-boosting exercises (though they may
improve one's test-taking skills, reduce nervousness, improve confidence,
etc.).

I'm sure you all know that the SAT Verbal + Math combined score was set up
to be 10 times the  standardized IQ? Thus, a combined score of 1400
represents an "IQ" of roughly 140. The less common ACT test made no such
attempt to hide the correlation, so a "140" on the ACT is deemed by
universities to be about equivalent to a 1400 on the SATs. (The test is
skewed toward only measuring the high end, it turns out. The "average"
combined score is around 950-1050, but these are for mostly college-bound
folk. If given to *everyone*, the average would be much lower. Thus, the
average of 1000 may more closely correspond to an "IQ" of 110, and scores
of 1400 or so may more closely correspond to IQs of 150 or higher.

IQ tests vary dramatically in their standard deviations, not to mention the
core issue of what it is they really test. A score of 130 on the
Stanford-Binet might equal a 150 on the Johns Hopkins Perceptual Test, a
1250 on the SAT, or even a 170 on the California Test of Mental Maturity.
Some tests have set arbitrary upper limits, like the 160 (= 1600) on the
SATs. 

About the alleged score of Marilyn vos Savant, a 230 or 240, I am
mystified--this sounds like some kind of oddball test (anybody have any
details?). I once saw an estimate of IQs of great scientists and scholars.
The estimates, based on anecdotal evidence of mental achievements (Mozart
writing music at age 3, etc.), placed Hans Bethe and Goethe up at around
205, with everyone else somewhat lower, sometimes much lower. I think
Einstein was around 150, which shows the pointlessness in many ways.

Having met my share of Mensans (and the even more insufferably pompous
International Legion of Intelligence folks), I can tell you they're overly
impressed with their own testing achievements (their vaunted "top 2%" is of
course a plebeian one out of 50, and probably one out of 10 college
students, one out of three science majors, and nearly everyone on this
list) and are usually not very interesting to talk to...witchcraft, cats,
and UFO theories seem to be their favorite topics. 

The Extropians I've met, in the Bay Area (the near Trascend?) and at
parties, are clearly a much more rarefied group.


-Tim May
