Ed Lane writes:
>Increasing IQ for IQ sake is something to impress (or bore) your
>friends with.  Kind of like body building ["beep-beep" digression alert!]

While I found Ed's posting interesting, I thought that I should point
out that body-building is probably something that extropians would
not find useless, at least until the age of decent medical care arrives.

Recent studies indicate that building muscle tissue has several health
benefits and can stave off many degenerative diseases, some of which
are now recognized as caused or exacerbated by loss of muscle mass as
the body ages. Apparently, of several kinds of people who engaged in
frequent exercise, the body builders showed a clear advantage in health
over the long term, even compared to runners, swimmers, or people doing
other kinds of aerobic activity. It seems that while aerobic exercise
is necessary, it is not sufficient if you want to get all of the
life-extension benefits of exercise. Additionally, (if I'm remembering
this correctly) every additional pound of muscle you carry requires an
additional 30 calories per day to maintain. Add ten pounds of muscle
and your maintenance caloric intake goes up by 300 calories every day.
That means you can stay slim more easily.

Currently, I'm trying to lose about twenty extra pounds of fat, primarily
with (usually) daily aerobic exercise (a lifecycle, in my case). Soon,
when I've advanced a little further in getting back my aerobic fitness,
I intend to spend more time in strength training.

Sure, when we've all uploaded this will become a useless activity, but
while we wear bodies, we may as well try to keep them in good shape.
After all, there's almost no telling at this point how long we'll
have to keep them.

