Date: 05 Apr 93 11:07:38 EDT
From: Duncan Frissell <76630.3577@CompuServe.COM>
Subject: Electron Mightier Than Sword

National Commie Radio this morning had a report on a trade battle going 
on in Canada.

As you may know, Canada bans a lot of publications in some cases to 
protect the domestic publishing industry.  Specifically, a foreign 
magazine may not carry any (or more than a certain number of?) Canadian 
ads.  This is supposed to support the Canadian magazine oligopoly which 
is able to charge high ad rates.  Publications violating these rules 
would be subject to seizure at the border.

Enter Time Canada which has started a publication called Sports 
Illustrated Canada.  The publication carries 1/3 original material on 
Canadian sports, 2/3 copy reprinted from Sports Illustrated.  It charges 
the lower US page rates for advertisers and has signed up many Canadian 
companies.  The government is supposed to decide today whether or not 
the publication is "Canadian" or "US."

Whatever the decision, it will be difficult to seize at the border.  
Sports Illustrated Canada is edited in New York and uploaded to a printing 
and distribution company in Canada.  The government may decide to seize 
the regular edition of Sports Illustrated instead which *does* cross the 
border.  Good news for Canadian printers!  

The government may also try to plug what it refers to as an electronic 
loophole in its customs regulations.  Wait until everyone in North 
America has a 1.5 megabit line to their homes.  Government monopolies 
will be hard put to stand in that wind.

